##### rule 9.1
summary: Reports a read of an automatic object that the control-flow analysis finds uninitialized on every path (certain) or on some path (possible).
limits: Only automatic scalar objects are tracked. Values written through a pointer, and elements of arrays, are not followed.

--- ok: initialized at the declaration
int f(void) { int x = 1; return x; }

--- ok: assigned on every path before the read
int f(int c)
{
    int x;
    if (c) { x = 1; } else { x = 2; }
    return x;
}

--- ok: initialized through its address
void init(int *p);
int f(void)
{
    int x;
    init(&x);
    return x;
}

--- bad: read of an object that was never written
int f(void)
{
    int x;
    return x; /*@V*/
}

--- bad: written on only one path
int f(int c)
{
    int x;
    if (c) { x = 1; }
    return x; /*@V*/
}

--- bad: read in an expression before the assignment
int f(void)
{
    int a;
    int b = a + 1; /*@V*/
    a = 2;
    return b + a;
}

##### rule 9.2
summary: Reports an aggregate initializer that omits the braces of a nested aggregate (the single {0} form is accepted).
limits: Only initializer lists are examined.

--- ok: fully braced
int a[2][2] = { { 1, 2 }, { 3, 4 } };

--- ok: the zero initializer
int b[2][2] = { 0 };
struct pair { int x; int y; } p = { 0 };

--- ok: braces for a nested struct
struct inner { int a; int b; };
struct outer { struct inner i; int c; };
struct outer o = { { 1, 2 }, 3 };

--- bad: nested array initialized without inner braces
int a[2][2] = { 1, 2, 3, 4 }; /*@V*/

--- bad: nested struct initialized without braces
struct inner { int a; int b; };
struct outer { struct inner i; int c; };
struct outer o = { 1, 2, 3 }; /*@V*/

##### rule 9.3
summary: Reports an array initializer list that supplies fewer elements than the array size, other than the single-zero form.
limits: String literal initializers of char arrays are not examined here.

--- ok: every element supplied
int a[3] = { 1, 2, 3 };

--- ok: the single zero form
int b[10] = { 0 };

--- ok: size taken from the initializer
int c[] = { 1, 2, 3, 4 };

--- bad: fewer elements than the array size
int a[5] = { 1, 2 }; /*@V*/

--- bad: a single non-zero element for a larger array
int b[4] = { 7 }; /*@V*/

##### rule 9.4
summary: Reports an element that is designated more than once in one initializer list.
limits: Only designated initializers are examined.

--- ok: distinct designators
int a[3] = { [0] = 1, [2] = 3 };
struct s { int x; int y; } v = { .x = 1, .y = 2 };

--- bad: the same array element twice
int a[3] = { [1] = 1, [1] = 2 }; /*@V*/

--- bad: the same member twice
struct s { int x; int y; };
struct s v = { .x = 1, .x = 2 }; /*@V*/

##### rule 9.5
summary: Reports an array of unspecified size that is initialized with array designators.
limits: Only arrays declared without a size are examined.

--- ok: sized array with designators
int a[5] = { [3] = 1 };

--- ok: unspecified size without designators
int b[] = { 1, 2, 3 };

--- bad: unspecified size with a designator
int a[] = { [2] = 1 }; /*@V*/

##### rule 10.1
summary: Subset: reports Boolean or enumerated operands of arithmetic operators, unary minus on unsigned operands, and operands of bitwise and shift operators that are not unsigned or not constants.
limits: Only the listed operator and operand combinations are examined, not the whole operand table.

--- ok: unsigned operands of bitwise operators
unsigned int f(unsigned int a, unsigned int b) { return (a & b) | (a << 1); }

--- ok: arithmetic on integers
int g(int a, int b) { return a + b * 2; }

--- bad: Boolean operand of an arithmetic operator
int f(_Bool flag) { return flag + 1; } /*@V*/

--- bad: enumerated operand of an arithmetic operator
enum e { A, B };
int g(enum e v) { return v + 1; } /*@V*/

--- bad: unary minus on an unsigned operand
int h(unsigned int u) { return (int)-u; } /*@V*/

--- bad: bitwise operator on signed non-constant operands
int k(int a, int b) { return a & b; } /*@V*/

--- bad: shift of a signed non-constant value
int m(int a) { return a << 2; } /*@V*/

##### rule 10.2
summary: Reports a character operand used inappropriately in an addition or subtraction.
limits: Only addition and subtraction are examined.

--- ok: a digit converted to a number and back
int to_int(char c) { return c - '0'; }
char to_char(int d) { return (char)('0' + d); }

--- ok: character compared and not computed
int is_a(char c) { return c == 'a'; }

--- bad: two characters added
int f(char a, char b) { return a + b; } /*@V*/

--- bad: a character added to a character constant
int g(char a) { return a + 'b'; } /*@V*/

##### rule 10.3
summary: Reports an assignment or initialization of a non-constant value to an object of a different essential type category or of a narrower width.
limits: Constants are exempt. Only assignments and initializers are examined; function arguments and returns are Rule 10.3's neighbours but are not covered here.

--- ok: same category, same or wider width
int f(int a) { long wide = a; int same = a; return (int)wide + same; }

--- ok: constants may initialize narrower objects
unsigned char u = 3;

--- ok: a conditional keeps the type of its operands
int g(int c, int a, int b) { int r = c ? a : b; return r; }

--- bad: narrowing assignment
void f(int i)
{
    unsigned char u;
    u = i; /*@V*/
    (void)u;
}

--- bad: different category (int to float)
void g(int i)
{
    float f = i; /*@V*/
    (void)f;
}

--- bad: integer to Boolean
void h(int i)
{
    _Bool b = i; /*@V*/
    (void)b;
}

--- bad: wider to narrower within the same category
void k(long l)
{
    int n = l; /*@V*/
    (void)n;
}

##### rule 10.4
summary: Reports arithmetic, relational, equality and bitwise operators whose non-constant operands have different essential type categories.
limits: Constants are exempt. The essential type of arithmetic on small integers is computed without promotion.

--- ok: same category
int f(int a, int b) { return a + b; }
int g(unsigned int a, unsigned int b) { return a < b; }

--- ok: a constant operand of another category
int h(unsigned int u) { return u + 1; }

--- ok: small unsigned arithmetic is unsigned
int k(unsigned char a, unsigned char b, unsigned char c) { return a == (b - c); }

--- bad: signed with unsigned
int f(int a, unsigned int b) { return a + b; } /*@V*/

--- bad: integer with floating
int g(int a, double b) { return a < b; } /*@V*/

--- bad: Boolean with integer in a comparison
int h(_Bool f, int a) { return f == a; } /*@V*/

##### rule 10.5
summary: Subset: reports casts to or from Boolean, to character, to an enumeration type from another type, and from character to floating.
limits: Numeric casts between integer and floating types are not reported. Whether a cast of an integer constant to char is reported depends on the strict reading (undecided, see docs/independent-validation.md).

--- ok: numeric casts
long f(int a) { return (long)a; }
unsigned int g(int a) { return (unsigned int)a; }
float h(int a) { return (float)a; }

--- ok: a cast between character types
char k(char c) { return (char)c; }

--- bad: unsigned char is an unsigned number in the essential type model, not a character
char u(unsigned char c) { return (char)c; } /*@V*/

--- bad: integer cast to Boolean
_Bool f(int a) { return (_Bool)a; } /*@V*/

--- bad: integer cast to character
char g(int a) { return (char)a; } /*@V*/

--- bad: integer cast to an enumeration
enum e { A, B };
enum e h(int a) { return (enum e)a; } /*@V*/

--- bad: character cast to floating
float k(char c) { return (float)c; } /*@V*/

##### rule 10.6
summary: Reports a composite arithmetic expression whose value is assigned to an object of a wider essential type.
limits: The essential type of the expression is that of its operands (no integer promotion).

--- ok: the operands are widened before the operation
int f(short a, short b) { int r = (int)a + (int)b; return r; }

--- ok: assignment to an object of the same width
short g(short a, short b) { short r = (short)(a + b); return r; }

--- bad: a short sum assigned to an int
int f(short a, short b)
{
    int r = a + b; /*@V*/
    return r;
}

--- bad: an unsigned char product assigned to an unsigned int
unsigned int g(unsigned char a, unsigned char b)
{
    unsigned int r;
    r = a * b; /*@V*/
    return r;
}

##### rule 10.7
summary: Reports a composite expression used as an operand together with an operand of a wider non-constant essential type.
limits: Constants are exempt.

--- ok: both operands of the same width
int f(short a, short b, short c) { return (a + b) * c; }

--- ok: a constant operand
int g(short a, short b) { return (a + b) * 2; }

--- bad: a composite short operand combined with an int
int f(short a, short b, int c) { return (a + b) * c; } /*@V*/

--- bad: a composite char-width operand combined with a long
long g(unsigned char a, unsigned char b, long c) { return (a + b) + c; } /*@V*/

##### rule 10.8
summary: Reports a cast of a composite expression to a wider or different essential type.
limits: Only explicit casts are examined.

--- ok: cast to the same width
short f(short a, short b) { return (short)(a + b); }

--- ok: cast of a non-composite operand to a wider type
int g(short a) { return (int)a; }

--- bad: composite short sum cast to int
int f(short a, short b) { return (int)(a + b); } /*@V*/

--- bad: composite unsigned char product cast to unsigned long
unsigned long g(unsigned char a, unsigned char b) { return (unsigned long)(a * b); } /*@V*/

##### rule 11.1
summary: Reports a cast that converts to or from a function pointer type, other than the ordinary conversion of a function designator to a pointer.
limits: A cast to void of a function pointer is not reported (documented gap; the FreeRTOS authors treat it as a violation).

--- ok: calling through a function pointer and assigning a compatible one
int twice(int x) { return x * 2; }
int (*fp)(int) = twice;
int use(void) { return fp(3); }

--- ok: attributes do not change the function pointer type
typedef void (*task_t)(void *arg);
static void idle(void *arg) __attribute__((noreturn));
static void idle(void *arg) { (void)arg; for (;;) { } }
task_t entry = &idle;

--- bad: function pointer cast to void pointer
void (*fp)(void);
void *p = (void *)fp; /*@V*/

--- bad: function pointer cast to an integer
void (*fp)(void);
unsigned long v = (unsigned long)fp; /*@V*/

--- bad: cast between two function pointer types
int (*a)(int);
long (*b)(long) = (long (*)(long))a; /*@V*/

--- gap: a cast of a function pointer to void
void (*fp)(void);
void f(void) { (void)fp; } /*@V*/

##### rule 11.2
summary: Reports a conversion between a pointer to an incomplete type and any other pointer or integer type (a null pointer constant is exempt).
limits: Only conversions of pointers to incomplete struct or union types are examined.

--- ok: a null pointer constant and equal types
struct opaque;
struct opaque *handle;
int is_set(void) { return handle != 0; }
struct opaque *copy(struct opaque *p) { return p; }

--- ok: conversion to void pointer of a complete type
int x;
void *v = &x;

--- bad: pointer to incomplete type cast to an integer
struct opaque;
unsigned long f(struct opaque *p) { return (unsigned long)p; } /*@V*/

--- bad: pointer to incomplete type cast to another pointer type
struct opaque;
char *g(struct opaque *p) { return (char *)p; } /*@V*/

##### rule 11.3
summary: Reports an explicit cast between pointers to different object types (void pointers are excluded).
limits: Casts to and from character-type pointers are reported (strict reading; Coverity and cppcheck do not report them, so this is undecided).

--- ok: same type, qualifier changes handled elsewhere
int *f(int *p) { return (int *)p; }

--- ok: conversion through void pointer is Rule 11.5
void *g(int *p) { return (void *)p; }

--- bad: int pointer to float pointer
float *f(int *p) { return (float *)p; } /*@V*/

--- bad: struct pointer to int pointer
struct s { int a; };
int *g(struct s *p) { return (int *)p; } /*@V*/

--- bad: int pointer to char pointer (strict reading)
char *h(int *p) { return (char *)p; } /*@V*/

##### rule 11.4
summary: Reports an explicit cast between a pointer and an integer type (a null constant is exempt).
limits: Only explicit casts are examined.

--- ok: null pointer constants
int *p = (int *)0;
void *q = (void *)0;

--- ok: no cast
int *r = 0;

--- bad: integer address cast to a pointer
int *p = (int *)0x1000; /*@V*/

--- bad: pointer cast to an integer
unsigned long f(int *p) { return (unsigned long)p; } /*@V*/

--- bad: a memory-mapped register address
#define REG (*(volatile unsigned int *)0x40021000)
unsigned int f(void) { return REG; } /*@V*/

##### rule 11.5
summary: Reports a conversion from pointer to void to a pointer to an object type, implicit or explicit.
limits: A conversion in the other direction (object pointer to void pointer) is compliant.

--- ok: object pointer to void pointer
int x;
void *v = &x;

--- ok: void pointer stays void pointer
void *copy(void *p) { return p; }

--- bad: implicit conversion
void *src(void);
int *p;
void f(void) { p = src(); } /*@V*/

--- bad: explicit cast of a void pointer
void *src(void);
int *g(void) { return (int *)src(); } /*@V*/

##### rule 11.6
summary: Reports an explicit cast between a pointer to void and an integer type.
limits: A null pointer constant is exempt.

--- ok: null pointer
void *p = (void *)0;

--- ok: pointers only
int x;
void *q = &x;

--- bad: integer cast to void pointer
void *p = (void *)5; /*@V*/

--- bad: void pointer cast to integer
unsigned long f(void *p) { return (unsigned long)p; } /*@V*/

##### rule 11.7
summary: Reports a cast between a pointer and a floating type.
limits: Compilers reject such casts, so the check is reached only through compiler extensions. No violating example can be compiled in standard mode.

--- ok: pointer and floating objects without a cast between them
float f;
int *p;
void g(void) { f = 1.0f; p = 0; }

--- ok: casts that are not between pointers and floating types
double d = (double)1;
long l = (long)d;

##### rule 11.8
summary: Reports an explicit pointer cast that removes const or volatile from the pointed-to type.
limits: Only explicit casts are examined; an implicit conversion that drops a qualifier is a compiler diagnostic.

--- ok: adding a qualifier
const int *f(int *p) { return (const int *)p; }

--- ok: casting without changing qualifiers
int *g(int *p) { return (int *)p; }

--- bad: casting away const
int *f(const int *p) { return (int *)p; } /*@V*/

--- bad: casting away volatile
int *g(volatile int *p) { return (int *)p; } /*@V*/

##### rule 11.9
summary: Reports a null pointer constant that is not written with the NULL macro (for example a plain 0 or (void *)0).
limits: NULL passed through the argument of another macro still counts as NULL.

--- ok: NULL and comparisons without a constant
#include <stddef.h>
int *p = NULL;
int f(int *q) { return q != NULL && *q > 0; }

--- ok: a pointer used as a condition
int g(int *q) { if (q) { return 1; } return 0; }

--- bad: zero as a null pointer
int *p = 0; /*@V*/

--- bad: comparison with zero
int f(int *p) { return p == 0; } /*@V*/

--- bad: a cast zero
void *q = (void *)0; /*@V*/

##### rule 12.1
summary: Strict reading: reports a binary operator that has an unparenthesized binary operand of a different precedence level.
limits: Chains of the same precedence level are accepted; this is the strict interpretation of the rule.

--- ok: explicit parentheses
int f(int a, int b, int c) { return a + (b * c); }

--- ok: operators of the same level
int g(int a, int b, int c) { return a + b + c; }
int h(int a, int b, int c) { return a * b * c; }

--- ok: parenthesized logical mix
int k(int a, int b, int c) { return (a && b) || c; }

--- bad: addition mixed with multiplication
int f(int a, int b, int c) { return a + b * c; } /*@V*/

--- bad: logical and mixed with logical or
int g(int a, int b, int c) { return a || b && c; } /*@V*/

--- bad: shift mixed with addition
int h(int a, int b) { return a << b + 1; } /*@V*/

##### rule 12.2
summary: Reports a constant shift count that is negative or not smaller than the width of the left operand.
limits: Only constant shift counts are examined.

--- ok: counts within the width
unsigned int a = 1U << 31;
unsigned char b(unsigned char x) { return (unsigned char)(x << 3); }

--- bad: count equal to the width
unsigned int f(unsigned int x) { return x << 32; } /*@V*/

--- bad: negative count
int g(int x) { return x << -1; } /*@V*/

--- bad: count too big for the type of the left operand
long long h(long long x) { return x >> 64; } /*@V*/

##### rule 12.3
summary: Reports every use of the comma operator.
limits: Commas that separate arguments, declarators and initializers are not the comma operator.

--- ok: commas in calls, declarations and initializers
int add(int a, int b);
int a = 1, b = 2;
int arr[3] = { 1, 2, 3 };
int s = 0;
void f(void) { s = add(a, b); }

--- bad: comma operator in a for statement
void f(void)
{
    int i, j;
    for (i = 0, j = 10; i < j; i++) { j--; } /*@V*/
}

--- bad: comma operator in an expression
int g(int a, int b) { return (a++, b); } /*@V*/

##### rule 12.4
summary: Reports a constant unsigned addition, subtraction, multiplication or shift whose exact result does not fit the type (wrap-around).
limits: Only constant expressions are evaluated.

--- ok: results that fit
unsigned int a = 5U - 3U;
unsigned int b = 0xFFFFFFFFU - 1U;
unsigned int c = 1U << 31;

--- bad: unsigned subtraction below zero
unsigned int a = 0U - 1U; /*@V*/

--- bad: unsigned addition past the maximum
unsigned int b = 0xFFFFFFFFU + 1U; /*@V*/

--- bad: unsigned multiplication overflow
unsigned int c = 0x10000U * 0x10000U; /*@V*/
